1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,403
The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

2
00:00:03,403 --> 00:00:08,354
So the farthest we can see should be thirteen point eight billion light years, right?

3
00:00:08,354 --> 00:00:08,704
Wrong.

4
00:00:08,704 --> 00:00:14,063
The edge of what we can see is about forty six billion light years away, in every direction.

5
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:17,616
More than three times farther than light could have traveled.

6
00:00:17,616 --> 00:00:21,810
Today we find out how that's possible, and how big everything really is.

7
00:00:22,510 --> 00:00:25,650
To feel it, we need a ruler that works out there.

8
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:26,611
Not kilometers.

9
00:00:26,611 --> 00:00:26,996
Light.

10
00:00:26,996 --> 00:00:31,417
Light moves at almost three hundred thousand kilometers every second.

11
00:00:31,417 --> 00:00:37,505
One light year, the distance it covers in a year, is about nine and a half trillion kilometers.

12
00:00:37,505 --> 00:00:39,555
Let's climb, one rung at a time.

13
00:00:40,256 --> 00:00:40,804
Rung one.

14
00:00:40,804 --> 00:00:41,353
The Moon.

15
00:00:41,353 --> 00:00:46,351
Light covers the distance from Earth to the Moon in about one point three seconds.

16
00:00:46,351 --> 00:00:50,069
Every Moon you have ever seen was a little over a second old.

17
00:00:50,769 --> 00:00:51,282
Rung two.

18
00:00:51,282 --> 00:00:51,739
The Sun.

19
00:00:51,739 --> 00:00:53,621
Eight minutes and twenty seconds.

20
00:00:53,621 --> 00:00:58,470
When you feel sunlight on your skin, it left the Sun before you finished your coffee.

21
00:00:59,170 --> 00:00:59,876
Rung three.

22
00:00:59,876 --> 00:01:01,736
Neptune, the last big planet.

23
00:01:01,736 --> 00:01:02,827
About four hours.

24
00:01:02,827 --> 00:01:08,152
A message sent to a spacecraft there at breakfast gets its answer back after lunch.

25
00:01:08,852 --> 00:01:09,487
Rung four.

26
00:01:09,487 --> 00:01:14,381
Voyager one, our most distant spacecraft, launched in nineteen seventy seven.

27
00:01:14,381 --> 00:01:17,940
This month, it reaches a distance of one full light day.

28
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:24,233
NASA's signal, moving at the fastest speed there is, will take twenty four hours just to get there.

29
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:26,474
Now the first giant jump.

30
00:01:26,474 --> 00:01:28,631
The nearest star, Proxima Centauri.

31
00:01:28,631 --> 00:01:30,604
Four point two four light years.

32
00:01:30,604 --> 00:01:34,610
Voyager would need more than seventy thousand years to get there.

33
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:38,864
Everything humans have ever built is still sitting at the front door.

34
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:40,137
Rung six.

35
00:01:40,137 --> 00:01:41,795
Our galaxy, the Milky Way.

36
00:01:41,795 --> 00:01:47,340
About one hundred thousand light years across, with at least one hundred billion stars.

37
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:55,435
Light takes so long to cross it that the starlight arriving from the far side today set off before humans had invented farming.

38
00:01:56,136 --> 00:01:56,799
Rung seven.

39
00:01:56,799 --> 00:02:01,687
Andromeda, the nearest big galaxy, about two and a half million light years away.

40
00:02:01,687 --> 00:02:04,764
On a dark night you can see it with your naked eye.

41
00:02:04,764 --> 00:02:10,557
That faint smudge is light that left when our ancestors were first learning to make stone tools.

42
00:02:11,257 --> 00:02:11,949
Rung eight.

43
00:02:11,949 --> 00:02:19,308
In twenty fourteen, astronomers mapped how galaxies flow through space and drew the borders of our home supercluster.

44
00:02:19,308 --> 00:02:22,893
They named it Laniakea, Hawaiian for immeasurable heaven.

45
00:02:22,893 --> 00:02:29,561
It spans about five hundred and twenty million light years and holds around one hundred thousand galaxies.

46
00:02:30,261 --> 00:02:31,438
And the final rung.

47
00:02:31,438 --> 00:02:33,669
The edge of the observable universe.

48
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:37,077
About forty six billion light years in every direction.

49
00:02:37,077 --> 00:02:40,794
Ninety three billion light years from one side to the other.

50
00:02:40,794 --> 00:02:42,963
Which brings us back to the puzzle.

51
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:48,415
How can anything be that far, in a universe only thirteen point eight billion years old?

52
00:02:49,115 --> 00:02:50,184
Here is the trick.

53
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:51,788
Space itself is stretching.

54
00:02:51,788 --> 00:02:54,282
Picture a photon leaving a distant galaxy.

55
00:02:54,282 --> 00:03:03,666
It races toward us at the speed of light, but the space it crosses keeps growing underneath it, like an ant walking on a balloon that someone keeps inflating.

56
00:03:03,666 --> 00:03:11,921
By the time the light finally arrives, after thirteen and a half billion years, the galaxy that sent it has been carried much farther away.

57
00:03:12,621 --> 00:03:14,052
This is a real example.

58
00:03:14,052 --> 00:03:19,964
In twenty twenty five, the James Webb Space Telescope confirmed a galaxy called MoM z fourteen.

59
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:23,511
Its light is about thirteen and a half billion years old.

60
00:03:23,511 --> 00:03:30,293
When that light left, the galaxy was only about two billion light years from the matter that would become us.

61
00:03:30,293 --> 00:03:37,325
Today, by our calculation using the standard cosmological model, it's about thirty four billion light years away.

62
00:03:38,025 --> 00:03:47,620
The oldest light of all is the cosmic microwave background, the afterglow of the Big Bang, released about three hundred and eighty thousand years after it began.

63
00:03:47,620 --> 00:03:51,912
The gas that glowed was then only around forty million light years away.

64
00:03:51,912 --> 00:03:56,024
That same matter is now about forty five billion light years from us.

65
00:03:56,024 --> 00:03:56,978
That's the edge.

66
00:03:56,978 --> 00:03:57,633
Not a wall.

67
00:03:57,633 --> 00:04:00,852
Just the farthest light that has had time to reach us.

68
00:04:01,551 --> 00:04:02,764
And it gets stranger.

69
00:04:02,764 --> 00:04:06,516
The universe is expanding, and the expansion is even speeding up.

70
00:04:06,516 --> 00:04:13,213
Today the rate is about seventy kilometers per second for every three point two six million light years of distance.

71
00:04:13,213 --> 00:04:16,330
Add enough distance, and the stretching outruns light.

72
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:22,507
Beyond roughly fourteen billion light years, galaxies are moving away from us faster than light, right now.

73
00:04:22,507 --> 00:04:24,239
That doesn't break relativity.

74
00:04:24,239 --> 00:04:26,086
Nothing is racing through space.

75
00:04:26,086 --> 00:04:27,472
Space itself is growing.

76
00:04:28,171 --> 00:04:30,029
Here's an embarrassing secret.

77
00:04:30,029 --> 00:04:32,754
We don't fully agree on that expansion rate.

78
00:04:32,754 --> 00:04:39,009
Measure it from the Big Bang's afterglow, as the Planck satellite did, and you get about sixty seven.

79
00:04:39,009 --> 00:04:46,750
Measure it from nearby exploding stars and pulsing stars, as the SH0ES team did with Hubble, and you get about seventy three.

80
00:04:46,750 --> 00:04:53,438
Webb has double checked the nearby side, and while teams still debate the details, the gap hasn't gone away.

81
00:04:53,438 --> 00:05:00,869
Astronomers call it the Hubble tension, and it might be a clue that something in our picture of the universe is missing.

82
00:05:01,569 --> 00:05:03,483
So there are really three borders.

83
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:10,010
The observable universe, at about forty six billion light years, holds everything whose light has reached us so far.

84
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:16,143
But there is a much smaller one, the cosmic event horizon, at roughly sixteen and a half billion light years.

85
00:05:16,143 --> 00:05:19,801
Light leaving a galaxy beyond it today will never, ever reach us.

86
00:05:19,801 --> 00:05:22,952
We can see galaxies out there, as they were in the past.

87
00:05:22,952 --> 00:05:26,103
But whatever they are doing now is gone from us forever.

88
00:05:26,803 --> 00:05:27,889
Play that forward.

89
00:05:27,889 --> 00:05:31,328
Every year, more distant galaxies slip past that horizon.

90
00:05:31,328 --> 00:05:44,601
Physicists Lawrence Krauss and Robert Scherrer worked out that in around a hundred billion years, every galaxy beyond our own local group will have been pushed past that horizon, and later they fade from view completely.

91
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:49,850
By then, the Milky Way and Andromeda will long since have merged into one giant galaxy.

92
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:55,943
Astronomers living there would look out and see a single island of stars in an empty, black universe.

93
00:05:55,943 --> 00:06:00,348
They would have no way to discover that the universe is expanding at all.

94
00:06:00,348 --> 00:06:04,450
We are lucky to be looking now, while the evidence is still visible.

95
00:06:05,150 --> 00:06:06,965
How much is inside that bubble?

96
00:06:06,965 --> 00:06:10,301
For years, the headline number was two trillion galaxies.

97
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:19,198
Then in twenty twenty one, NASA's New Horizons spacecraft, far beyond Pluto where the sky is darker, measured how much background light there really is.

98
00:06:19,198 --> 00:06:22,944
The verdict: hundreds of billions of galaxies, not two trillion.

99
00:06:22,944 --> 00:06:30,494
Still, if you counted one galaxy every second, without sleeping, two hundred billion would take you more than six thousand years.

100
00:06:31,194 --> 00:06:34,838
Numbers this big stop meaning anything, so let's shrink it.

101
00:06:34,838 --> 00:06:38,853
Squeeze the entire observable universe down to the size of Earth.

102
00:06:39,553 --> 00:06:43,976
On that scale, our whole Milky Way would be about fourteen meters across.

103
00:06:43,976 --> 00:06:47,309
Roughly a city bus, floating somewhere inside a planet.

104
00:06:47,309 --> 00:06:51,853
Andromeda would be a few blocks away, about three hundred and forty meters.

105
00:06:51,853 --> 00:06:55,731
Proxima Centauri would sit about half a millimeter from the Sun.

106
00:06:55,731 --> 00:06:56,882
And the Sun itself?

107
00:06:56,882 --> 00:06:58,518
Smaller than a single atom.

108
00:06:59,218 --> 00:07:01,095
So is that the whole universe?

109
00:07:01,095 --> 00:07:02,409
Almost certainly not.

110
00:07:02,409 --> 00:07:06,664
The observable universe is only the part we can see, centered on us.

111
00:07:06,664 --> 00:07:08,666
Every galaxy has its own bubble.

112
00:07:08,666 --> 00:07:16,925
An alien astronomer forty billion light years away sees a completely different edge, and would look at us as the ones at the border.

113
00:07:17,625 --> 00:07:19,299
What lies past our horizon?

114
00:07:19,299 --> 00:07:21,532
The best clue is the shape of space.

115
00:07:21,532 --> 00:07:26,741
The Planck satellite measured that space is flat, to within a fraction of a percent.

116
00:07:26,741 --> 00:07:32,943
If the universe curves back on itself at all, it must be enormously larger than the part we can see.

117
00:07:32,943 --> 00:07:37,656
And if it is perfectly flat, the simplest answer is the most unsettling one.

118
00:07:37,656 --> 00:07:38,958
It may go on forever.

119
00:07:39,658 --> 00:07:41,748
So, how big is the universe, really?

120
00:07:41,748 --> 00:07:45,405
The part we can see is ninety three billion light years across.

121
00:07:45,405 --> 00:07:47,959
The part we could ever reach is far smaller.

122
00:07:47,959 --> 00:07:52,138
And the whole thing might have no size at all, because it may never end.

123
00:07:52,838 --> 00:07:55,538
Next time, we stop looking and start falling.

124
00:07:55,538 --> 00:07:58,117
What happens if you fall into a black hole?

125
00:07:58,117 --> 00:08:01,056
Spoiler: it depends on which black hole you pick.

126
00:08:01,056 --> 00:08:03,876
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

127
00:08:03,876 --> 00:08:04,956
See you out there.
